      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is what introduces semi-transparent pixels when the alpha-modulating color filter triggered a resolve before the erode/dilate operated. At this point we\u0027ve lost the paint anti-aliasing status that came in from the original draw call.\n\nFrom an animation/stability/quality POV, anti-aliasing these passes leads to much better results and once we land on the graphite model of always AA, there won\u0027t be as many surprises like this.\n\nBut maybe we do need to reconsider how all of this logic interacts with deferred effect sampling. Even if the paint were anti-aliased, the deferred nearest-neighbor sampling that is seen by the erode/dilate filter does not apply any partial coverage at the edges of the image. I think this issue gets wrapped up in b/40042624, which let us remember in the FilterResult that we are on fractional pixel coordinates. Right now everything always rounds out to integers ASAP.",
